{#
  Measurement, in one file, included last in the body of every page.

  The site shipped with none of this and the only question Ava will ever ask
  about her website is whether it is bringing her work. Two things answer it:
  how many people arrive, and how many of them do something.

  THE TOKENS LIVE IN _data/analytics.json, NOT site.json, and that is
  deliberate. The admin panel writes site.json back with only the fields
  admin/config.yml declares, so a key it has never heard of is dropped the
  first time Ava saves her hours — the tracking would go quiet weeks later
  with nothing obvious to point at. analytics.json is not a CMS collection
  and nothing but a deploy can touch it.

  Both blocks render nothing when their value is empty, so a build made
  before an ID exists ships a clean page rather than a beacon posting to
  nowhere.
#}

{#
  Cloudflare Web Analytics — traffic.

  Cookieless and aggregated on Cloudflare's side: it sets nothing on her
  visitors' devices. Kept alongside GA4 rather than replaced by it because
  it is the honest number for "how many people came", it costs one small
  request, and it keeps reporting if the GA property is ever misconfigured.

  Public by design; the token identifies the site to Cloudflare and is
  visible in the HTML of every page. It is not a credential.
#}
{%- if analytics.cloudflareToken %}
<script type="module" src="https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js" data-cf-beacon='{"token": "{{ analytics.cloudflareToken }}"}'></script>
{%- endif -%}

{#
  Google Analytics 4 — conversions.

  Added for one reason Cloudflare cannot do: events. Cloudflare Web Analytics
  has no event model at all, so a tap on a tel: link was uncountable, and the
  phone is how most of Ava's customers actually book. GA4 counts it.

  This one DOES set cookies, and that is a real change to what this site was:
  it used to carry no third-party state at all, which is why there is no
  consent banner. No US federal law requires one here and this business is
  far below every CCPA threshold, so the banner is still not required — but
  the claim "this site sets nothing" is no longer true, and the privacy line
  in the footer says so rather than leaving the old comment lying.

  Measurement ID, not a secret: it is in the HTML of every page by design.
#}
{%- if analytics.ga4MeasurementId %}
<script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id={{ analytics.ga4MeasurementId }}"></script>
<script>
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
  gtag('js', new Date());
  gtag('config', '{{ analytics.ga4MeasurementId }}');
</script>
{#
  Loaded only when GA4 is on, because it has nothing to report to otherwise.
  It lives in js/ rather than inline here so it follows the same rule as
  every other script on this site: one IIFE per file, progressive, and
  harmless when the thing it depends on is missing.
#}
<script src="/js/call-tracking.js"></script>
{%- endif -%}
